      <div>
        <h2>Add a host from its own terminal.</h2>
        <p class="sub">Run <strong>mpx bind</strong> on the machine. It offers itself with a QR code right in the terminal and an announcement on your local network; scan it or type the PIN, the machine confirms on its own screen, and it lands on the wall.</p>
        <ul class="feat-list">
          <li><b>No secrets typed, none pasted</b>Multiplex generates a fresh ed25519 key per host and sends only the public half; the private key never leaves your device.</li>
          <li><b>Reversible by name</b>The enrolled key is labeled in authorized_keys, so <span class="mono">mpx unbind</span> removes exactly what bind added &mdash; nothing else.</li>
          <li><b>One small CLI</b>Prebuilt for macOS and Linux: Homebrew, a one-line installer, or a checksummed download &mdash; the <a href="mpx/">install guide</a> covers all three.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ══ BENTO ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="wrap rev">
      <h2>And the parts you will lean on daily.</h2>
      <div class="bento">
        <div class="cell c-mosh">
          <h3>mosh built in <span class="pro">PRO</span></h3>
          <p>Flip a host to mosh and the session survives sleep, roaming, and IP changes. A native Swift mosh client, no companion app: made for a headset that suspends and an iPad that hops networks.</p>
          <div class="wire">SSH &#8594; mosh-server &#8594; <b>UDP 60001</b> &middot; reconnects itself after standby</div>
        </div>
        <div class="cell c-file">
          <h3>Drop a file, get a path</h3>
          <p>Drag a file onto the terminal, or attach from Files, Photos, or the iPad camera. Multiplex uploads it beside your agent and types the remote path for you.</p>
          <code>~/project/.multiplex-drops/spec.pdf</code>
        </div>
        <div class="cell c-view">
          <h3>&#8983;&#8202; An inline browser, docked to the session</h3>
          <p>Confirm a link your dev server printed and it opens as a browser tab right beside the terminal. A <span class="mono">localhost</span> URL in a pane is the <em>host&rsquo;s</em> loopback, so Multiplex reopens it via the host&rsquo;s own address &mdash; your dev server, viewed from the headset. Viewport tabs merge and split like terminals, live page intact.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cell c-fv">
          <h3>&#9636;&#8202; Files and git diffs, one tab over</h3>
          <p>Long-press a path in the terminal &mdash; or open a file viewer tab &mdash; to browse the pane&rsquo;s directory over SFTP: code with syntax highlighting, Markdown, images, and git diffs that keep updating while your agent edits. Review the change without leaving the window.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cell c-termcell">
          <div>
            <h3>A real SSH terminal</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><b>xterm-256color</b>, mouse-aware TUIs</li>
              <li><b>IMEs and hardware keyboards</b></li>
              <li><b>tmux menu</b> and touch copy mode</li>
              <li><b>Ten themes</b>, custom editor with Pro</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <figure>
            <img src="assets/ipad-terminal.png" width="3200" height="580" alt="The bottom of a Multiplex terminal on iPad: tmux status line, the agent command strip, and the TALLY key rail" loading="lazy">
          </figure>
        </div>
        <div class="cell c-widget">
          <h3>Widgets &amp; Shortcuts</h3>
          <p>Home Screen widgets show last-known session frames with an honest SEEN stamp. Siri and Shortcuts open a shell or launch an agent in a chosen working directory, optionally running a host-configured setup script before an optional first prompt.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cell c-priv">
          <h3>Private by design</h3>
          <p>Hosts and secrets sync through iCloud Keychain, end to end encrypted.</p>
          <span class="mono">NO ACCOUNT<br>NO ANALYTICS<br>NO SERVERS</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

